  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two conceptual pathways for managing a mountain or plateau ecosystem under climate change. In both cases, climate change drives cryosphere thaw, which then affects biogeochemical cycling across a landscape with mountains, snow, and a lake. A gauge in the upper right indicates overall impact: it is higher in the top scenario and lower in the bottom one.\n\nThe upper panel shows mainly damaging pressures and limited recovery. Visible drivers include nitrogen fertilizers, overgrazing, and construction, with only a small restorative action near the lake. The color bar below runs from red/orange toward blue, suggesting these activities range from harmful to somewhat mitigating, with restoration as the main positive action.\n\nThe lower panel presents a more integrated, lower-impact strategy. Construction is still present, but it is paired with restoration and additional measures such as sustainable grazing, ecological compensation, clean energies, and science/technology innovation, while nitrogen fertilizer use remains part of the system. The arrows imply these actions interact with cryosphere thaw and nutrient cycling. Overall, the message is that combining restoration with broader sustainable management appears to reduce climate-related ecosystem impacts compared with the more conventional pressure-heavy scenario.",
